<section style="padding:0 24px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto 56px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 18px;">How to read the prices on this page</h2>
    <div class="rich"><p>Every number here comes from a Calgary firm's own website, read on 30 September 2026. Where the firm says its prices are before GST, the table says ex GST; where the page is silent, it says GST not stated and you should ask. GST is 5% and Alberta has no PST.</p><p>Several firms publish different numbers for the same job on different pages. Martini Stone Masonry gives one per-square-foot band on its cost page and a different one on its repair page, and Calgary Parging's service page gives a wider range than its itemised cost guide. This page uses the itemised, dated figure and names the firm, so you can see whose number it is.</p><p>Calgary Parging says its ranges rest on more than 1,360 written estimates, and Martini says its page draws on 1,200 or more estimates and invoices, some from Cochrane, Priddis and Canmore. Those are the firms' own statements and have not been audited here. Alberta advises getting more than one written estimate, so treat these as questions to put to a contractor, not as a quote.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="padding:0 24px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto 56px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 18px;">What moves a stucco quote up or down</h2>
    <div class="rich"><p>Two walls with the same crack can quote very differently. These are the drivers Calgary firms name, with the figures they publish:</p><ul><li><strong>Height and scaffolding.</strong> Calgary Parging says upper-storey or peaked sections need scaffolding, typically $4,000 to $12,900 ex GST. It also prices a second-storey woodpecker patch above a first-storey one ($800 against $600 on its 2024 list).</li><li><strong>Colour matching.</strong> Calgary Parging says a colour match adds $230 to $575 ex GST. The manufacturer Imasco says colour can vary with weather, texture, job conditions and method of application, and that one colour batch should cover the whole job.</li><li><strong>Hidden water damage.</strong> Calgary Parging says water-damaged sheathing must be replaced first and adds $575 to $2,300 or more ex GST. Calgary Stucco and Slate notes that nobody can tell you the full extent until the wall is opened up.</li><li><strong>Minimum call.</strong> Calgary Parging states a minimum service call of $550 to $690 ex GST, so on that firm's list even a very small job starts there.</li><li><strong>System and finish.</strong> Cement, acrylic and EIFS walls are repaired differently, and Martini's per-hole woodpecker price is for EIFS. Say which you have when you call.</li><li><strong>Season.</strong> Calgary Parging says stucco cures best above 5&deg;C in dry conditions, typically April through October, and that off-season repairs require heated hoarding.</li></ul><p>Read more on the <a href="/hail-woodpecker-stucco-damage/">hail and woodpecker damage page</a> and see how patches hold up on the <a href="/stucco-repair-calgary/">stucco repair page</a>.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="padding:0 24px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto 56px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 18px;">Whole walls, recoats and re-stucco</h2>
    <div class="rich"><p>Once a job covers a whole wall the figures climb quickly. Calgary Parging lists $9,650 for a 9 by 30 ft wall removed and replaced, and Martini lists $14,350 for one elevation with the brick stripped and an acrylic finish, both ex GST. For whole houses, a Calgary contractor's 2024 price list from Calgary Parging shows $12,000 to $18,000 for an acrylic-finish bungalow, $18,000 to $33,000 for a mid-size house and $30,000 to $60,000 for a larger one, GST not stated.</p><p>Per-square-foot figures differ even more. Martini's summary of its estimates puts a full application at $14.50 to $18 per sq ft, plus $4.50 where cement board is needed, ex GST; the rows behind that summary are tagged to Canmore, not Calgary. Total Exteriors advertises $8 to $15 per sq ft for repair and $25 to $50 for full replacement, GST not stated. Calgary Stucco and Slate frames $5,000 to $20,000 and up as a very rough guide for full re-coats or re-stuccos. None of these firms prices a recoat of a whole stucco house apart from a full re-stucco, so ask for it as its own line on the quote. See the <a href="/re-stucco-calgary/">re-stucco page</a> for how contractors scope a full wall.</p><p>EIFS jobs sit in a different bracket: Calgary Parging lists about $62,100 to remove and make safe roughly 1,400 sq ft of EIFS panel, and Martini lists $36,800 to tear off siding and fit an EIFS system, both ex GST. Two jobs have no reliable Calgary price on file: stucco removal and stucco painting. The only Calgary removal figure found sat on a 2020 page from a firm whose prices contradict each other from page to page, and Calgary Parging's painting page gives no number, so neither is shown here.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="padding:0 24px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto 56px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 18px;">Hail, insurance, permits and timing</h2>
    <div class="rich"><p>The Insurance Bureau of Canada says hail damage is typically covered by home insurance policies, so check your own policy. The City of Calgary advises contacting your home insurance provider before any repair work, and IBC suggests photographing the damage and keeping receipts. A stucco contractor can give you a written repair estimate that you may pass to your insurer. This site does not file, negotiate or manage claims, and a price on this page is not a claim amount. There is more on the <a href="/hail-woodpecker-stucco-damage/">hail damage page</a>.</p><p>For permits, the City of Calgary says most repair or replacement work does not need a development or building permit, and it lists a building permit for siding with a different material. Stucco is not named on that page, so confirm your project with the City on 311. If your home is newer, check your new home warranty before paying for repairs.</p><p>Timing changes price and scheduling. Imasco's specification rules out stucco work below 4&deg;C for 24 hours before, during and after application, ADEX asks for 5&deg;C or above for at least 24 hours, and Calgary Parging says off-season work needs heated hoarding. If you are calling between November and March, ask which work can go ahead now, which waits for spring, and whether the quote includes hoarding. Call (403) 555-0148 to describe your wall.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="padding:72px 24px;background:#F3ECE1;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;">
    <span class="kicker">FAQ</span>
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(24px,3.5vw,36px);margin:0 0 32px;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px;">
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E3D8C6;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:700;color:#2A2521;margin:0 0 10px;">How much does stucco repair cost in Calgary?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">There is no single Calgary price, because each firm publishes its own. Calgary Parging lists a small patch at $550 to $1,125 ex GST, and Calgary Stucco and Slate gives about $300 to $1,500 for small crack and spot repairs as a very rough guide. Larger jobs run into the thousands: Calgary Parging lists a 9 by 30 ft wall replaced at $9,650 ex GST. Only a contractor who has seen your wall can give you a real quote.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E3D8C6;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:700;color:#2A2521;margin:0 0 10px;">How much does it cost to fix a woodpecker hole in stucco?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">Calgary Parging's 2024 price list shows $600 for a first-storey woodpecker patch and $800 for second storey and up, GST not stated. Martini Stone Masonry lists $250 to $350 per hole on EIFS, depending on height and access, GST not stated. These are what those firms advertise, not a market rate. The hail and woodpecker page explains what a contractor looks at before pricing.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E3D8C6;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:700;color:#2A2521;margin:0 0 10px;">Are these prices before or after GST?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">It depends on the firm. Calgary Parging says its cost-guide prices are before 5% GST, and Martini's cost page says its figures exclude GST. Other firms do not say, so ask each contractor whether the quote includes GST. Alberta has no PST.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E3D8C6;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:700;color:#2A2521;margin:0 0 10px;">Will my insurance pay for stucco repair after hail?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">The Insurance Bureau of Canada says hail damage is typically covered by home insurance policies, but your own policy decides. The City of Calgary advises contacting your insurer before doing any repair work. A contractor can give you a written estimate, and you deal with your insurer directly. This site does not file, negotiate or manage insurance claims.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E3D8C6;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:700;color:#2A2521;margin:0 0 10px;">Can stucco be repaired in a Calgary winter?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">Imasco says to do no stucco work below 4°C for 24 hours before, during and after application, and ADEX asks for 5°C or above for at least 24 hours. Calgary Parging says stucco cures best above 5°C, typically April through October. Off-season repairs need heated hoarding, which can change the quote. Ask the contractor what can be done now and what waits for spring.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
